# LINE ENDINGS. Every commit in this repo printed a wall of
#
#   warning: in the working copy of '<file>', LF will be replaced by CRLF
#            the next time Git touches it
#
# because this machine's git carries `core.autocrlf = true` (from
# C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig -- the Git for Windows installer's own
# system config, not a repo choice) while every tool that writes files here --
# the agent editors, python, the codegen -- writes LF. `core.safecrlf`
# defaults to `warn`, so git said so on every add. The warning was correct and
# useless: nothing was ever lost, and it buried real output.
#
# WHAT THIS CHOOSES, and why not the other spellings:
#
#   * text=auto eol=lf   <- this file
#         Normalise to LF in the object store (which is already what is
#         stored: autocrlf has been converting on the way in for the whole
#         life of the repo, so NO BLOB CHANGES and no history is rewritten),
#         and check out LF in the working tree. An LF file then round-trips
#         with no conversion, so no warning.
#   * text=auto eol=crlf / plain `text=auto`
#         Keeps the CRLF working tree, so it keeps the warning: the tools
#         still write LF and checkout would still put the CRLF back.
#   * `* -text` (no conversion at all)
#         Would compare the CRLF working tree byte-for-byte against LF blobs
#         and report EVERY TRACKED FILE AS MODIFIED. Not a candidate.
#
# ONE STEP IS OWED PER CHECKOUT, once. A checkout made before this file
# existed still holds CRLF files, and adding one of those (an edit that
# preserved its line endings) warns the other way round -- "CRLF will be
# replaced by LF". Refresh the working tree from the index. It changes no
# blob, no commit and no file CONTENT -- only the bytes between the lines:
#
#     git rm --cached -r -q . && git reset --hard
#
# `git checkout-index -f -a` is the gentler-looking spelling and it is not
# enough: measured here 2026-09-02, it rewrote one file out of 2694 and left
# every entry whose cached stat still matched the index untouched. Refuse it.
# The same line is in docs/current/operations/worktrees.md.
#
# NOTHING HERE NEEDS CRLF. There is no tracked .bat, .cmd or .sln. The .ps1
# build scripts are read by Windows PowerShell 5.1, which parses LF fine (what
# it cannot do is guess a non-ASCII encoding without a BOM -- that is
# validate.ps1's S8 rule, and it is about bytes, not line breaks).
* text=auto eol=lf

# The tracked binaries: live-capture PNGs under review/qa/. `text=auto` would
# detect these by content anyway; they are named so a future reader does not
# have to trust the detector.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
